
== nsh 0.5.1 (224d9a7)

== Generate throwaway keys (kept out of git under .nsh/keys)
$ nsh keygen .nsh/keys/root
key 2c886d4c900a2d7e: .nsh/keys/root.secret (keep it secret), .nsh/keys/root.pub
$ nsh keygen .nsh/keys/release
key 87b971b7cdef15b3: .nsh/keys/release.secret (keep it secret), .nsh/keys/release.pub

== Certify the release key for this app family, 90 days
$ nsh certify --root .nsh/keys/root.secret --key .nsh/keys/release.pub --apps com.example.nshstarter.* --days 90 -o .nsh/keys/release.cert
.nsh/keys/release.cert: key 87b971b7cdef15b3 certified by root 2c886d4c900a2d7e for com.example.nshstarter.* until 90 days from now

== Publish the package (signed with the release key)
$ nsh publish App.tsx --key .nsh/keys/release.secret --cert .nsh/keys/release.cert -o .nsh/out/com.example.nshstarter.remote-1.nshpkg
warn app id `com.example.nshstarter.remote` is a placeholder: give the app an id under a domain you own before releasing it, as apps with one id share their data
✓ built App.tsx (100.2 KB js, 134.8 KB bytecode, 30ms)
.nsh/out/com.example.nshstarter.remote-1.nshpkg: com.example.nshstarter.remote 1.0.0 (build 1), 0 assets, 746 KB, signed by 87b971b7cdef15b3, for runtime 0.5.1 or newer

== Verify against the root (as a host would)
$ nsh verify .nsh/out/com.example.nshstarter.remote-1.nshpkg --root .nsh/keys/root.pub
valid: com.example.nshstarter.remote 1.0.0 (build 1), release key 87b971b7cdef15b3, sha256 d551d74f6fa8136098d87983f4358ddeaabf4ff753171fa2503937bc1aa3c1d1, 0 assets, bytecode for this runtime

== Revoke this package: sequence 1, or the next one over the last list
$ nsh revoke --root .nsh/keys/root.secret --sequence 1 --package d551d74f6fa8136098d87983f4358ddeaabf4ff753171fa2503937bc1aa3c1d1 -o .nsh/delivery/revocations.json
.nsh/delivery/revocations.json: list 1, 0 release keys and 1 packages revoked

